<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01934</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4249f7f9-5695-46cf-8a8f-211307fdd018</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="92d59337-3613-48cb-ba17-d11f71cb511e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>true statements about the graph that represents \(y=2x(x-11)\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Its \(x\)-intercepts are at (0,0) and (11,0).The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: Its \(x\)-intercepts are at (0,0) and (11,0). The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e72a5230-87d9-4225-b0fc-194d6408af7d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> equations whose graphs have a vertex with \(x\)-coordinate 2.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=(x-1)(x-3)\) and \( y=x(x-4)\) </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: Its \(x\)-intercepts are \(y=(x-1)(x-3)\), \(y=x(x-4)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="1e77c74e-9e17-4703-b6f8-9cb42b073a3c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Three different equations are given. Refer to these equations to complete questions a–f.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Equation</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-intercept</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-coordinate of the vertex</emphasis></entry></row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=x(x-2)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>a. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>b. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>c. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>d. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=-5x(3-x)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>e. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>f. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0, 2)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((2, 0)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0,-2)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((-2,0)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((0,0)\) and \((2,0)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((0,0)\) and \((2,0)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="66b54e19-d36a-4965-a9d9-1e3943173cdb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1</para><para> Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 1. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="37652284-a5ba-426c-839a-e975a25748c9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)?</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para><para>\((0,-5)\) and \((0,4)\)</para><para>\((5,0)\) and \((-4,0)\)</para><para>\((0,5)\) and \((0,-4)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4cac9766-6f18-4bcb-9ab9-7099ea97c8cf" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)? </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! -0.5</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is -0.5. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="ee4f8051-bec4-4711-b7e4-0d39d4a1875d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)?</para>
        <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0,-3)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((-3,0)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\)</para><para>\((0,0)\) and \((0,3)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="f5018e25-e97d-48c6-b4e2-1557f6575971" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)? </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1.5</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 1.5. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="ddcb53cd-215f-4142-a282-3ad0c570fa80" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>What is the equation of the axis of symmetry of \(y = (x - 4)(x + 5)\)? (If needed, enter fractional values as a decimal.)</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! -0.5</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is -0.5. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="693ff91a-6e94-4ba9-b34f-7bd5d58db355" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Answer questions a–c about the function \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\).</para>
      <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the vertex of the graph \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para><para>\((0,2)\) and \((0,4)\)</para><para>\((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\)</para><para>\((0,-2)\) and \((0,-4)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="8376bb58-0f6a-4f6c-8301-ebf69e1887f9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Is \((-1,3)\) a point on the graph?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! Substituting -1 for \(x\) gives \((-1-2)(-1-4)\) or -3 -5, which is 15, so \((-1,15)\) would be a point on the graph, not \((-1,3)\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para><para>The correct answer is No. Substituting -1 for \(x\) gives \((-1-2)(-1-4)\) or \(-3 \cdot  -5\), which is 15, so \((-1,15)\) would be a point on the graph, not \((-1,3)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="70f17ceb-fe72-4533-98be-ce8ace7c8884" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Does the graph represent the equation \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\)? 
            
            <figure><media alt="Graph of nonlinear function, origin O. Horizontal axis from negative 8 to 6, by 2’s. Vertical axis from negative 8 to 12, by 4’s. Line passes through negative 7 comma 8, negative 5 comma 0, negative 3 comma 0 and 1 comma 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d7ede3e12cc95715ca6ed8c75079c4081888c1a"/></media></figure></para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.11.4.</para><para>The correct answer is No. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
